      {"name": "boreal-lake-plain", "note": "Beyond the ice sheets in the taiga, GUM's wind-blown units under wet or bleached soils are the lake and river plains of West Siberia and the Russian north (lacustrine-alluvial silt, clay and sand), not loess (West Siberia read half loess, 7 November).", "nearRiver": false, "absLat": [55, 75], "fields": [{"field": "soil_group", "values": ["Albeluvisols", "Gleysols", "Stagnosols", "Planosols"]}], "units": {"Ed": "lake", "Eu": "lake", "Er": "lake", "Ea": "lake"}},
      {"name": "eolian-silt", "nearRiver": false, "fields": [{"field": "soil_group", "values": ["Chernozems", "Kastanozems", "Phaeozems", "Cryosols", "Luvisols", "Cambisols", "Albeluvisols", "Calcisols", "Solonetz", "Planosols", "Gleysols", "Vertisols", "Stagnosols", "Umbrisols"]}, {"field": "clay_pct", "min": 12}],
       "units": {"Ed": "loess", "Eu": "loess", "Er": "loess", "Ea": "loess"}},
      {"name": "crest-glaciated", "note": "Knolls and steep slopes the ice scoured (at least 8 m above the mean ground within the relief radius, or steeper than 0.35): a skin of till on rock. Peat, dunes and loess keep their own recipe (relief thins them).",
